-- D6 tier D (judiciary), second slice: intermediate appellate courts, the
-- 42 states (of 50) that have at least one -- 8 states (Delaware, Maine,
-- Montana, New Hampshire, Rhode Island, South Dakota, Vermont, Wyoming)
-- genuinely have no intermediate tier at all (confirmed via search, not
-- assumed from the comparison chart's own silence). Same shape as
-- migration 069 (state supreme courts) -- small hand-verified data, not
-- an ingester, and the same owner decision from that migration carries
-- forward: retention elections are modeled as is_elected=TRUE.
--
-- Classification source: Ballotpedia's "Intermediate appellate courts"
-- comparison chart (same one-page-covers-every-state format as the
-- Supreme Court chart), cross-checked against migration 069's own
-- established 16-state Supreme Court retention list (Alaska, Arizona,
-- California, Colorado, Florida, Indiana, Iowa, Kansas, Maryland,
-- Missouri, Nebraska, Oklahoma, South Dakota, Tennessee, Utah, Wyoming)
-- rather than trusting this chart's own wording distinctions -- "Assisted
-- appointment" vs plain "Gubernatorial appointment" turned out NOT to
-- reliably signal whether retention applies (California's intermediate
-- courts are labeled the latter but ARE genuinely subject to retention
-- elections, confirmed directly via a live search before trusting it).
-- 14 of the 16 retention states have an intermediate appellate court
-- (South Dakota and Wyoming don't have this tier at all).
--
-- This chart also proved genuinely incomplete, the same way the Supreme
-- Court chart omitted Maryland: Maryland's Appellate Court (15 judges,
-- assisted appointment with Senate confirmation, confirmed subject to
-- retention elections) is missing from this chart entirely and was added
-- by hand after confirming it directly via the court's own page.
--
-- Excluded, same discipline as migration 069: Connecticut, Hawaii,
-- Massachusetts, New Jersey, and New York's intermediate courts are pure
-- gubernatorial/commission appointment with no subsequent voter role at
-- all (matching those same states' Supreme Courts, already excluded
-- entirely). South Carolina and Virginia elect their intermediate court
-- BY THE LEGISLATURE, not by voters -- same exclusion already applied to
-- those states' Supreme Courts and to legislature-selected executive
-- offices throughout this project. North Dakota's "Court of Appeals" is
-- not a standing court with distinct judges at all -- panels are drawn
-- temporarily from sitting trial judges for up to a year at a time, so
-- there's no separate elected/appointed office to model.
--
-- Two real, disclosed gaps: Michigan (25 judges) and the Pennsylvania
-- Superior Court (15 judges) are missing a current-roster table on
-- Ballotpedia entirely -- confirmed by direct inspection, not a parsing
-- failure on this project's end (a further targeted search for an
-- alternate roster page also came up empty). Left out of this migration
-- rather than guessed; 40 of the tier's ~875 judges, flagged for a
-- follow-up pass with a different source.
--
-- Real per-court roster pages come in two different Ballotpedia table
-- formats, both handled: the newer "Office/Name/Party/Date assumed
-- office" format already used for Supreme Courts and executive offices,
-- and an older "Judge/Tenure/Appointed By" format with no party column at
-- all (party is genuinely unavailable from this source for those judges,
-- not the same thing as a nonpartisan election -- disclosed as such in
-- each affected judge's bio rather than silently defaulted). In the
-- older format, "Appointed By" is literally the word "Elected" for a
-- judge who won their seat outright, or a specific governor's name for
-- one currently serving an interim appointment pending their own
-- election or retention vote -- mapped to how_obtained the same way
-- migration 069 mapped its own "Appointed by" column.
--
-- A multi-district/division court (Missouri's 3 districts, Washington's
-- 3 divisions) puts one SEPARATE roster table per district on the same
-- page rather than one consolidated table -- caught and fixed before
-- generating: an early version of this migration's own scraper only
-- captured the first such table, silently returning Missouri at 14 of 32
-- judges and Washington at 10 of 22. Fixed to concatenate every matching
-- table on a page, re-verified against the correct total judge counts
-- (Missouri 31 of 32 -- one real vacancy; Washington exactly 22 of 22).
--
-- Arizona's Court of Appeals came back with 27 real, individually-named,
-- non-duplicate judges against the comparison chart's stated "22" --
-- trusted the court's own live roster page over the aggregate chart,
-- same precedent as Maryland's supreme court term-length data earlier
-- this session (a specific primary source outranks a summary chart).
--
-- Real vacancies preserved, not filled, same discipline as every prior
-- migration in this project (spot-checked: Illinois's First District
-- Appellate Court page includes an explicit "Vacant" row alongside 24
-- real names, correctly excluded rather than counted as a 25th judge).



